      body: () => `
        <div class="kena-lead itihasa-lead">
          <p>The <i>Mahābhārata</i> is a Sanskrit <i>itihāsa</i> built around the struggle between two branches of the Kuru dynasty, but the received work is far larger than a war epic. Its eighteen parvans contain genealogy, myth, pilgrimage, ritual, law, royal policy, ascetic teaching, yoga, theology and sustained argument about <i>dharma</i>. The traditional scale is roughly 100,000 <i>ślokas</i>; the <i>Harivaṃśa</i>, devoted largely to genealogy and Kṛṣṇa, is commonly transmitted as a supplement rather than one of the eighteen parvans.${C(1,25)}</p>
          <p>The epic is deliberately framed as a sequence of recitations. Ugraśravas Sauti tells it to sages at Naimiṣa after hearing Vaiśampāyana’s recitation at King Janamejaya’s snake sacrifice; within that history Vyāsa is both ancestral figure and traditional composer. The result is not merely a story being narrated once, but a story presented as remembered, transmitted and interpreted by successive audiences.${C(2,3)}</p>
          <p>Its moral world is correspondingly difficult. The central catastrophe does not arise because one side possesses all virtue and the other all vice. Oaths, kinship, kingship, marriage, loyalty, truth, violence and renunciation repeatedly collide. The long postwar books matter because the epic refuses to let victory settle the questions that produced the war in the first place.${C(1,14,15)}</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="kena-toc itihasa-toc" aria-label="Contents"><div class="kena-toc-title">Contents</div><ol>
          <li><a href="#mbh-formation">Formation and textual history</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mbh-frame">Narrative frame and Kuru genealogy</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mbh-parvans">The eighteen parvans</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mbh-embedded">Embedded works and intellectual material</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mbh-critical">Manuscripts and the Critical Edition</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mbh-commentaries">Commentarial and later reception</a></li>
          <li><a href="#itihasa-notes">Sources</a></li>
        </ol></nav>

        <section class="kena-section" id="mbh-formation"><h2>Formation and textual history</h2>
          <p>The Mahābhārata belongs to a different kind of textual history from the Vedas. Vedic recitation aimed at extremely exact preservation; epic reciters worked in a more flexible narrative environment in which diction, explanation and episode could change as the story moved through time and region. This is why the oldest recoverable Mahābhārata is not identical with a hypothetical first performance. The manuscript evidence is abundant but comparatively late, while external references and early fragments show that recognizable Mahābhārata material circulated long before the medieval codices from which a complete text can be reconstructed.${C(2)}</p>
          <p>The epic itself distinguishes a <i>Bhārata</i> of 24,000 verses from additional material, and later tradition also speaks of <i>Jaya</i>, <i>Bhārata</i> and <i>Mahābhārata</i> as successive scales of the work. That three-stage model is useful as a traditional account of growth, but it should not be mistaken for three surviving editions that can simply be placed side by side. The current text is the outcome of a long redactional history in which narrative frames, didactic collections and devotional material became part of the architecture of the whole.${C(2)}</p>
          <p>The Spitzer manuscript is important because its ancient table of contents does not list Virāṭa and Anuśāsana as separate parvans. That is evidence for the instability of parvan organization at an early date, not a license to delete those books from the received epic: both are firmly represented in the later manuscript tradition and in the Critical Edition.${C(2,6,15)}</p>
        </section>

        <section class="kena-section" id="mbh-frame"><h2>Narrative frame and Kuru genealogy</h2>
          <p>The outer frame begins at Naimiṣa forest, where Sauti recounts a story already performed at Janamejaya’s snake sacrifice. That inner recitation is significant because Janamejaya is a descendant of the protagonists: the war has become ancestral history, and hearing it is part of understanding how his own dynasty reached the present. The snake-sacrifice frame also absorbs the Āstīka story and gives the opening book its characteristic movement between remote myth, family genealogy and the immediate Kuru past.${C(3)}</p>
          <p>The dynastic crisis begins before the Pāṇḍavas and Kauravas are born. Śāntanu’s marriage to Gaṅgā produces Devavrata, later Bhīṣma; his catastrophic vow of celibacy makes possible Śāntanu’s marriage to Satyavatī but removes the strongest heir from succession. After Vicitravīrya dies childless, Vyāsa fathers Dhṛtarāṣṭra, Pāṇḍu and Vidura through niyoga. Pāṇḍu’s curse then removes him from ordinary procreation, while Kuntī’s mantra produces sons through deities and also explains the secret birth of Karṇa before her marriage.${C(1,3)}</p>
          <p>The younger generation inherits these unresolved questions. Dhṛtarāṣṭra is elder but blind; Pāṇḍu becomes king but withdraws; Yudhiṣṭhira is Pāṇḍu’s eldest acknowledged son; Duryodhana is Dhṛtarāṣṭra’s eldest. Their rivalry is therefore political before it is personal. The attempted burning of the Pāṇḍavas in the lac house, Draupadī’s marriage to all five brothers, and the establishment of Indraprastha temporarily create a second Kuru center rather than resolving the dispute.${C(1,3)}</p>
        </section>

        <section class="kena-section" id="mbh-parvans"><h2>The eighteen parvans</h2>
          <h3>1. Ādi Parvan</h3>
          <p>The opening book is itself a miniature library. Its traditional organization has nineteen sub-parvans and 236 chapters; the Critical Edition has 225 chapters. It establishes the Naimiṣa and snake-sacrifice frames, supplies Bhārata and Bhṛgu genealogies, tells of Śakuntalā and Bharata, follows Śāntanu, Gaṅgā, Satyavatī and Bhīṣma, and then moves into the births, education and rivalry of the Kuru princes. The lac-house plot, the Pāṇḍavas’ escape, encounters during their wandering, Draupadī’s svayaṃvara and the extraordinary decision that she marry all five brothers bring the book to the threshold of a divided Kuru polity.${C(3)}</p>
          <h3>2. Sabhā Parvan</h3>
          <p>Maya builds Yudhiṣṭhira’s great assembly hall at Indraprastha, and the new court becomes the stage for imperial ambition. Nārada’s questioning of Yudhiṣṭhira ranges through administration, ministers, espionage, war, taxation, agriculture and the treatment of vulnerable subjects. The rājasūya then raises Yudhiṣṭhira to imperial status and culminates in Kṛṣṇa’s killing of Śiśupāla. Duryodhana’s humiliation and resentment prepare the dice match, where Śakuni plays on Yudhiṣṭhira’s weakness for gambling until kingdom, wealth, brothers, self and Draupadī are staked. The legal and moral question posed by Draupadī in the hall—whether Yudhiṣṭhira retained the right to stake her after losing himself—remains deliberately difficult.${C(4)}</p>
          <h3>3. Vana Parvan</h3>
          <p>The twelve-year forest exile is the epic’s great expansion chamber: traditionally twenty-one parts and 324 chapters, reduced to sixteen parts and 299 chapters in the Critical Edition. The Pāṇḍavas move through forests and tīrthas while sages and visitors tell stories that interpret their predicament. Nala and Damayantī turns gambling, exile and recovery into a mirror for Yudhiṣṭhira; Sāvitrī’s recovery of Satyavān from Yama presents another model of fidelity and intelligence; the Rāmopākhyāna places a compressed Rāma story inside the epic; Arjuna’s acquisition of divine weapons and the Yakṣa’s interrogation of Yudhiṣṭhira prepare both martial and ethical return.${C(5)}</p>
          <h3>4. Virāṭa Parvan</h3>
          <p>The thirteenth year must be spent unrecognized. Yudhiṣṭhira becomes Kanka, Bhīma the cook Ballava, Arjuna the dance teacher Bṛhannalā, Nakula and Sahadeva take charge of horses and cattle, and Draupadī serves Queen Sudheṣṇā as Sairandhrī. The Kīcaka episode makes Draupadī’s vulnerability central when the powerful commander harasses her and Bhīma kills him secretly. The final cattle raid forces Arjuna to recover his weapons and reveal his identity while defending Virāṭa’s kingdom. Traditional editions have seventy-two chapters; the Critical Edition sixty-seven.${C(6)}</p>
          <h3>5. Udyoga Parvan</h3>
          <p><i>Udyoga</i> means effort, and the effort here is first diplomatic. The exile has ended; the Pāṇḍavas seek restoration, embassies move between courts, allies choose sides, and Kṛṣṇa himself goes to Hastināpura in a final attempt to prevent war. The book is intellectually dense before a weapon is raised. Vidura-nīti gives Dhṛtarāṣṭra a sustained discourse on prudent rule and ethical judgment, while the Sanatsujātīya turns to death, knowledge and liberation. The failure of negotiation makes the coming battle a political choice, not an accidental eruption.${C(7)}</p>
          <h3>6. Bhīṣma Parvan</h3>
          <p>The war begins under Bhīṣma’s command. Sanjaya, granted extraordinary sight, narrates events to the blind Dhṛtarāṣṭra. Before battle, the book pauses for descriptions of the earth and cosmic geography, then turns to Arjuna’s collapse when he sees teachers, elders and relatives across the field. The Bhagavad Gītā answers that crisis through competing and finally coordinated languages of action, knowledge, discipline and devotion. Once fighting begins, Bhīṣma dominates the first ten days until the Pāṇḍavas exploit his refusal to fight Śikhaṇḍin and Arjuna brings him down onto the bed of arrows.${C(8)}</p>
          <h3>7. Droṇa Parvan</h3>
          <p>Droṇa takes command on day eleven, and the war becomes progressively less governable. The traditional book has 204 chapters; the Critical Edition 173. Abhimanyu enters the cakravyūha and is surrounded and killed; Arjuna then vows to kill Jayadratha before sunset. The fourteenth day extends into night, Ghaṭotkaca’s devastating attack forces Karṇa to expend the divine weapon he had preserved for Arjuna, and the death of Droṇa follows the morally compromised announcement concerning “Aśvatthāman.” The parvan is one of the clearest places where the epic shows rules of combat collapsing under revenge and necessity.${C(9)}</p>
          <h3>8. Karṇa Parvan</h3>
          <p>Karṇa becomes commander for days sixteen and seventeen. The book compresses the war around his long-delayed confrontation with Arjuna while also dwelling on exhaustion, insult and the fraying discipline of both armies. Śalya serves as Karṇa’s charioteer but repeatedly undercuts him. Karṇa’s curses and earlier losses of divine protection become narratively active in the final duel; when his chariot wheel fails and he is unable to recover his position, Arjuna kills him at Kṛṣṇa’s urging. Traditional editions count ninety-six chapters, while the Critical Edition has sixty-nine.${C(10)}</p>
          <h3>9. Śalya Parvan</h3>
          <p>The eighteenth day destroys what remains of organized Kaurava resistance. Śalya becomes commander and is killed; Sahadeva kills Śakuni; Duryodhana withdraws to a lake and is discovered. The closing mace duel between Bhīma and Duryodhana brings the war’s oldest personal hatred to its conclusion when Bhīma strikes Duryodhana below the waist. The book traditionally has sixty-five chapters and the Critical Edition sixty-four. Its victory is already poisoned: only a tiny number of major warriors remain, and Aśvatthāman’s desire for retaliation points directly into the next parvan.${C(11)}</p>
          <h3>10. Sauptika Parvan</h3>
          <p>The “Book of the Sleepers” is the revenge after the formal war. Aśvatthāman, Kṛpa and Kṛtavarman attack the Pāṇḍava camp at night. Dhṛṣṭadyumna, Śikhaṇḍin, the sons of Draupadī and many other survivors are killed while sleeping or unable to mount an organized defense. The book’s eighteen chapters are retained in the Critical Edition, making the episode structurally stable even as its action deliberately violates the heroic codes repeatedly discussed earlier in the war.${C(12)}</p>
          <h3>11. Strī Parvan</h3>
          <p>The perspective moves from warriors to the bereaved. Dhṛtarāṣṭra, Gāndhārī, Kuntī, Draupadī and the women of the destroyed houses confront the bodies and consequences of Kurukṣetra. Funeral rites and consolatory discourses by Vidura and Vyāsa occupy the same book as raw lamentation. Gāndhārī’s grief culminates in a curse directed toward Kṛṣṇa and the Yādavas, giving the later destruction of Kṛṣṇa’s own clan an ethical connection to the war. The twenty-seven chapters are common to the traditional and critical structures.${C(13)}</p>
          <h3>12. Śānti Parvan</h3>
          <p>The longest parvan begins because Yudhiṣṭhira does not experience victory as triumph. He wants to renounce the throne, and the dying Bhīṣma becomes teacher. The book is organized around Rājadharma, Āpaddharma and Mokṣadharma: normal kingship and government; conduct under emergency conditions when ordinary norms break down; and a huge anthology of teachings on renunciation, self, cosmology, yoga and liberation. Traditional editions have 365 chapters; the Critical Edition has 353 and roughly 13,006 verses. The scale alone shows why describing Śānti merely as “advice on kingship” misses most of the book.${C(14)}</p>
          <h3>13. Anuśāsana Parvan</h3>
          <p>Bhīṣma’s instruction continues, but the emphasis shifts toward gifts, duties, religious observance, social relations and the religious economy of merit. Traditional editions count 168 chapters; the Critical Edition 154 and about 6,493 verses. The Viṣṇu Sahasranāma occurs here, embedded in the larger discussion of worship and praise. The parvan ends with Bhīṣma choosing the time of his death after the sun’s northward turn, and Gaṅgā mourning her son.${C(15)}</p>
          <h3>14. Āśvamedhika Parvan</h3>
          <p>Postwar sovereignty must be ritually reconstituted. Yudhiṣṭhira performs the horse sacrifice, while Arjuna follows the wandering horse and fights or negotiates with rulers along its route. This is not simply a return to prewar royal ritual: the campaign repeatedly reunites Arjuna with the human cost and unfinished political consequences of Kurukṣetra. The Anugītā is also placed here, framed by Arjuna’s admission that he no longer fully retains the teaching heard on the battlefield. Traditional editions have ninety-six chapters; the Critical Edition ninety-two.${C(16)}</p>
          <h3>15. Āśramavāsika Parvan</h3>
          <p>Fifteen years of Yudhiṣṭhira’s reign pass before Dhṛtarāṣṭra, Gāndhārī and Kuntī retire to the forest. Vidura dies after a final encounter with Yudhiṣṭhira. In the Putradarśana section, Vyāsa grants the bereaved a temporary reunion with warriors killed at Kurukṣetra, turning memory itself into a ritual event. Nārada later reports the deaths of Dhṛtarāṣṭra, Gāndhārī and Kuntī in a forest fire. The traditional text has thirty-nine chapters; the Critical Edition forty-seven.${C(17)}</p>
          <h3>16. Mausala Parvan</h3>
          <p>Thirty-six years after the war, the curse on the Yādavas comes to fruition. Intoxicated fighting at Prabhāsa becomes mutual slaughter with iron clubs; Balarāma departs, Kṛṣṇa is fatally struck by the hunter Jarā, and Dvārakā is submerged. Arjuna discovers that his former martial power no longer functions as it once did when he attempts to protect the survivors. The book is short—nine traditional chapters, eight in the Critical Edition—but it removes Kṛṣṇa and the Yādava world that had sustained the Pāṇḍavas through the central epic.${C(18)}</p>
          <h3>17. Mahāprasthānika Parvan</h3>
          <p>The Pāṇḍavas relinquish the kingdom and begin the “great departure.” Draupadī falls first, followed by Sahadeva, Nakula, Arjuna and Bhīma; Yudhiṣṭhira explains each fall in moral terms and continues without turning back. Only a dog remains with him. When Indra offers ascent on condition that the animal be abandoned, Yudhiṣṭhira refuses to betray a dependent companion. The dog is revealed as Dharma. With only three chapters, this is the epic’s shortest parvan.${C(19)}</p>
          <h3>18. Svargārohaṇa Parvan</h3>
          <p>The final book refuses a simple reward scene. Yudhiṣṭhira is disturbed to see former enemies in heaven and is then shown an apparent hell where the voices of those he loves are suffering. He chooses their company over personal bliss; the scene is disclosed as a final test, and the distinctions created by earthly hatred are ultimately dissolved. Traditional editions have six chapters and the Critical Edition five.${C(20)}</p>
        </section>

        <section class="kena-section" id="mbh-embedded"><h2>Embedded works and intellectual material</h2>
          <p>The Mahābhārata’s embedded texts are not detachable ornaments. They are usually introduced because a character needs a precedent, an argument or another conceptual language. Nala’s gambling and recovery interpret Yudhiṣṭhira’s disaster; Sāvitrī confronts death in a narrative told during a period of loss; Vidura-nīti and Sanatsujātīya are spoken while Dhṛtarāṣṭra still has a chance to alter policy; the Bhagavad Gītā occurs at the instant Arjuna refuses the role the entire political history has forced upon him.${C(5,7,8)}</p>
          <p>Śānti and Anuśāsana transform the epic into a vast compendium after the battlefield has fallen silent. Rājadharma considers the machinery and moral purpose of kingship; Āpaddharma asks what remains of duty when ordinary rules cannot be followed; Mokṣadharma collects competing ascetic, philosophical and yogic teachings. The Nārāyaṇīya situates Nārāyaṇa theology inside this postwar search for a highest good, while the Viṣṇu Sahasranāma places liturgical praise inside Bhīṣma’s final teaching. The Anugītā then revisits the problem of philosophical instruction under completely different circumstances, after the urgency of Kurukṣetra has passed.${C(14,15,16)}</p>
        </section>

        <section class="kena-section" id="mbh-critical"><h2>Manuscripts and the Critical Edition</h2>
          <p>No surviving manuscript can simply be designated “the original Mahābhārata.” Regional manuscript traditions differ in wording, chapter division and sometimes substantial passages. The problem faced by modern editors was therefore genealogical: which readings were shared because they descended from an older recoverable textual state, and which were later developments within particular branches of transmission? The distinction between northern and southern materials is useful, but neither is a single manuscript or perfectly uniform recension.${C(2)}</p>
          <p>The Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute began the Critical Edition project in 1919. V. S. Sukthankar became general editor in 1925 and established the method used for the opening book; later editors continued the work parvan by parvan until completion in 1966. The resulting Mahābhārata occupies roughly 13,000 pages in nineteen volumes, with the Harivaṃśa edited separately and index volumes added afterward.${C(21,1)}</p>
          <p>The editorial aim was deliberately modest in one important sense. Sukthankar rejected the fantasy of reconstructing an untouched authorial autograph from a fluid epic tradition. The attainable object was, in his formulation, the “oldest form of the text” recoverable from the manuscript evidence. A passage omitted from the constituted text was therefore not automatically declared worthless, false or religiously illegitimate; many such passages were preserved in the critical apparatus or appendices because they belonged to the documented history of the epic even when they could not be placed in the reconstructed common text.${C(2)}</p>
          <p>This is also why chapter and verse numbers vary between editions. Ādi has 236 chapters in the traditional count but 225 in the Critical Edition; Droṇa falls from 204 to 173; Karṇa from 96 to 69; Śānti from 365 to 353; Anuśāsana from 168 to 154. A serious citation therefore needs an edition or numbering system. “Mahābhārata 12.300,” without saying which text is being followed, can conceal a real bibliographic problem.${C(3,9,10,14,15)}</p>
        </section>

        <section class="kena-section" id="mbh-commentaries"><h2>Commentarial and later reception</h2>
          <p>The Sanskrit commentary tradition did not treat the epic as transparent. At least six commentators are known to have written across all eighteen parvans. Vimalabodha concentrated on difficult verses; Sarvajña Nārāyaṇa is known largely through later quotation; Arjuna Miśra wrote the <i>Bhāratārthadīpikā</i>; Ratnagarbha’s readings survive through extensive citation; Vādirāja Tīrtha produced the Dvaita-oriented <i>Lakṣālāṅkāra</i>; and Nīlakaṇṭha Caturdhara’s seventeenth-century <i>Bhāratabhāvadīpa</i> became the most influential comprehensive commentary associated with the northern vulgate.${C(22)}</p>
          <p>The received epic also moved far beyond Sanskrit manuscript culture. Episodes entered regional literature, performance, temple and pilgrimage traditions, ritual drama and modern theatre, novels, film and television. That reception should not be confused with the textual history of the Sanskrit Critical Edition, but neither is it secondary in the life of the Mahābhārata: the epic has always existed as a transmitted narrative world as well as a reconstructable Sanskrit text.${C(1,23,24)}</p>
        </section>
      `
